FILE: app/src/skip-to-end.ts
```ts
import type { AnimationGroup } from "@babylonjs/lite";
import { goToFrame } from "@babylonjs/lite";

export function skipToEnd(group: AnimationGroup): void {
    group.loopAnimation = false;
    const frameRate = group.frameRate ?? 60;
    goToFrame(group, group.duration * frameRate);
}
```

I set `loopAnimation` to false so that `goToFrame` clamps the time to the clip's end rather than wrapping it back to the start, then called `goToFrame` at the final frame, which pauses the group, sets `currentTime` to the duration, and writes the last keyframe values immediately; on every subsequent frame the paused group keeps writing that same pose. I was slightly unsure whether `goToFrame` reads `loopAnimation` directly from the group at call time or defers to the next update tick, but the notes describe `goToFrame` as writing the value at once, so I expect it to honour the flag I set just before.
